{"t":1793240782,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"23a28f09-669b-4eaf-9b7e-f819816cc47a","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T00:45:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 290045\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n845 PM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nPrevious forecast remains on track.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Areas of fog tonight into Thursday morning. \n \n- 2) Above normal temperatures through the end of the week, \n  with increasing clouds heading into the weekend. \n\n- 3) On-and-off rain showers possible Sunday night \nthrough Tuesday.\n\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Areas of fog tonight into Thursday morning. \n\nCombined with overall light winds, areas of dense fog could \nsettle in late tonight into Thu morning. Fog will likely be \nconcentrated along and east of the Blue Ridge through 14Z, with\nareas further west likely seeing overall patchier fog. As a \ndisturbance transitions offshore, clouds clear in the afternoon,\nwith warm afternoon temps ranging in the low- to-mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures through the end of \nthe week, with increasing clouds heading into the weekend. \n\nAs the midweek disturbance transitions offshore, another brief \nperiod of upper-level ridging builds over the Mid- Atlantic \nbetween Thu afternoon and Fri. Winds could gust up to 15-20 \nknots throughout the day on Fri as temps range well above \naverage with afternoon highs in the mid-70s. \n\nAn incoming cold front could bring overnight temps back down \ninto the 40s between Fri & Sat across the metros, with temps \npotentially in the upper 30s over the mountains. Sat will see \nincreasing cloud cover throughout the day as this front moves \nthrough the region, bringing temps back down to more seasonable\ndaytime ranges in the 60s. No precipitation is anticipated for \nmost of the weekend as this front slowly begins to track through\ninto early next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...On-and-off rain showers possible Sunday night \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nHigh pressure should keep most, if not all, of the region dry \nthrough the heart of the day Sunday. Rain chances could arrive with \nan approaching low pressure system Sunday night and linger through \nTuesday. It is too early to tell how much in coverage these showers \nwill be each period, but some areas may not see any showers at all. \nAll in all, rain amounts could be light. Temperatures will be \nseasonable Sunday, but then inch their way upwards Monday and \nTuesday as a southerly to southwesterly wind brings in slightly \nmilder air. Highs Sunday in the middle 60s for most locations, then \nclimb into the upper 60s to near 70 for both Monday and Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nAdditional low clouds and fog could develop late tonight into early\nThursday morning, with higher potential east of the Blue Ridge.\nThere is still some uncertainty as to where exactly the low \nclouds develop, so some TAF sites show borderline IFR/MVFR \nconditions between 0800Z-1400Z Thursday depending on this \nuncertainty. Generally lowered CIGs/VSBYs with 00Z TAFs given\ntrends. Fog could occasionally bring visibilities to 1-2SM \nduring this time.\n\nVisibilities return to VFR between 14Z-16Z Thursday, with \nclouds generally clearing by the afternoon. Winds flow southerly\nThursday before shifting northwesterly on Friday, with gusts \npotentially up to 15-20 knots throughout the day. Winds diminish\non Saturday before shifting southerly again. \n\nVFR conditions Sunday through Monday night. Light southerly winds \naround 5 knots or so Sunday, becoming light and variable southeast \nto northeast Monday and Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight southeasterly winds through this evening, then shifting \nsoutherly overnight. Winds strengthen to borderline SCA thresholds \nFriday & Saturday due to increased vertical mixing over near- \nshore/narrower waterways, shifting northwesterly by Friday evening. \nWinds diminish by Saturday evening. Up until Friday morning, winds \nstay within 5-10 knots. \n\nNo marine hazards expected Sunday through Monday night. Winds south \n5 to 10 knots Sunday, becoming east to northeast 5 to 10 knots \nMonday and Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nOnshore flow Thursday & Friday could push sensitive shorelines close \nto flood stage, primarily during the evening tide cycles. A Coastal \nFlood Advisory has been issued until 11PM this evening for Annapolis \nin anticipation of minor flooding that could occur with the high \ntide.\n\nLight offshore flow should lower tidal anomalies on Saturday before \nwinds shift again on Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory until 11 PM EDT this evening for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KLW/CPB/SRT\nAVIATION...KLW/CPB/SRT\nMARINE...KLW/CPB/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793240739,"days":{"2026-10-28":{"hi":null,"lo":49,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Cloudy then Areas Of Fog"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":73,"lo":52,"pop":1,"short":"Areas Of Fog then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":75,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":65,"lo":47,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":7,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":69,"lo":52,"pop":14,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":70,"lo":53,"pop":17,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":70,"lo":null,"pop":23,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793239200,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,10100,8800,9500,10100,10800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,9.7,9.8,9.5,9.4,9.4,9.0,6.3,4.3,2.1,1.6,1.2,2.0,5